<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="policies-and-approvals"
      updated="August 22, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"how-decisions", "How a decision is made"},
        {"first-policy", "A sensible first policy"},
        {"editing", "Editing the policy"},
        {"scoping", "Scope it to a runner or group"},
        {"approvals", "Approvals"},
        {"standing-grants", "Standing grants"},
        {"reason", "The reason requirement"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="policies-and-approvals">
        <:dek>
          Every run dispatch passes through your account's policy before it's sent to a
          runner. The policy makes one of three decisions: run it, pause it for approval, or
          deny the execution.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_h2 id="how-decisions">How a decision is made</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each pack action declares its risk tier — neither the caller nor the control plane can
        change it. A policy then decides whether the run is dispatched, paused for approval,
        or denied.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The policy is selected by scope, most specific first: the runner's own ruleset, then
        its group's, then the account default.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A policy can define per-action overrides. It walks the ordered list of action ID
        patterns — for example,
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">nomad.job_*</code>
        requires approval, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">*.delete_*</code>
        denies. The first match wins, so put the most specific patterns first.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        If no override matches, the action's risk tier defaults
        (<.docs_risk tier="low" /> / <.docs_risk tier="medium" /> / <.docs_risk tier="high" /> /
        <.docs_risk tier="critical" />) make the decision. In the policy these decisions are
        ><code class="text-brand-300">allow</code>, <code class="text-amber-300">require_approval</code>,
        and <code class="text-red-400">deny</code>. When the policy cannot answer — a missing
        policy, an unknown tier — it denies.
      </p>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="The shipped default.">
        New accounts allow low and medium risk, require approval for high, and deny critical
        — no overrides.
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="first-policy">A sensible first policy</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Keep the shipped defaults, then add overrides where real use shows you need them:
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          Run a week with the defaults. Watch the
          <a
            href={~p"/app/approvals"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Approvals</a>
          queue — the requests
          that keep showing up are your override candidates.
        </li>
        <li>
          Deny anything you never want an agent to touch
          (<code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">*.purge_*</code>
          → <code class="text-red-400">deny</code>) — a written rule beats everyone
          remembering.
        </li>
        <li>
          Turn repeated approvals into expiring standing grants instead of
          <code class="text-brand-300">allow</code>
          overrides — a grant stays tied to one key and expires on its own.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An override can also remove waiting. Some reads are high risk because their output can
        leak secrets — a full config dump may contain a credential — so the shipped policy
        holds them for a person. If your configs hold no secrets, or the exposure is
        acceptable, allow the action:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="Policy → overrides">overrides:
  - name: <span class="text-brand-300">allow-journal-reads</span>
    action: <span class="text-brand-300">linux.journalctl*</span>
    decision: <span class="text-brand-300">allow</span>
  - name: <span class="text-brand-300">allow-nginx-config-dump</span>
    action: <span class="text-brand-300">nginx.config_dump</span>
    decision: <span class="text-brand-300">allow</span></.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Redaction still masks credential patterns it recognizes, but it can miss — treat the
        output as sensitive when you allow a full dump.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="editing">Editing the policy</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open
        <a
          href={~p"/app/policies"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Policies</a>
        in the console sidebar. Owners and admins can edit; operators and viewers can read.
        The editor shows the four tier menus above the ordered override list. A higher tier can
        never be more permissive than a lower one — the editor and the API both refuse it.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/policy-editor.webp"
        title="emisar console · Policy"
        caption="Risk-tier defaults, the per-action override list, and approval requirements in one editor."
        alt="The Policy page in the emisar console: risk-tier defaults (Low and Medium set to allow, High to require approval, Critical to deny), the per-action override list, and the approval-requirements panel."
      />
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The preview runs your edited rules against the scope's real catalog, so you see each
        action's decision before saving.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Saving a change bumps the policy version and applies from the next dispatch. Every
        save writes an audit event, and every run records the policy version that gated it —
        so you can always see which rules were in force.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="scoping">Scope it to a runner or group</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The default policy governs the fleet. When one runner or group needs different rules —
        a locked-down database host, a permissive staging group — add a targeted ruleset for it
        in <a
          href={~p"/app/policies"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Policies</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A ruleset <strong class="text-zinc-200">replaces</strong>
        the default policy for its runner or group — what you see for a scope is exactly what
        runs there. Remove it and the scope falls back to the next-broader policy.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:tip} title="Pattern: let an agent iterate on staging, hands-off">
        <p>
          Give the <strong class="text-zinc-200">staging</strong>
          group a ruleset that sets every tier to
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs text-brand-300">allow</code>
          with no approvals. An agent can reproduce a bug, test a fix, and verify the result
          without waiting — and the production change it hands you is still held for approval
          by your stricter default.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="approvals">Approvals</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        When the decision is <code class="text-amber-300">require_approval</code>, the run is
        held and an approval request is created. Owners, admins, and operators get an email
        with a direct link, and the request appears under <strong>Approvals</strong>
        in the console with a live badge.
      </p>
      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/loop/approval-pending.webp"
        preview_h="max-h-[32rem]"
        title="emisar console · Approval"
        caption="The approver sees the action, risk, runner, exact command, and reason — one click approves or denies, optionally with a reuse window."
        alt="A pending approval for caddy.reload_config on edge-fra-01 requested by Maya Chen via Claude Code: the high risk tier, the exact command the runner will execute, the reason and the policy that held it, an expiry countdown, a reuse-window selector, and Approve and send / Deny buttons."
      />
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            One approval releases a run by default. For destructive work, require
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">two approvers</strong>
            with self-approval off — the requester cannot approve their own request.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Undecided requests <strong class="text-zinc-200">expire after 24 hours</strong> and
            the held run is cancelled.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            An LLM waiting on an approval long-polls
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">wait_for_run</code>
            and picks the result up the moment a human decides.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Each vote and final decision is audited with its approver, run ID, decision, and reason.
            Follow the run ID for the requester, action, arguments, and dispatch reason.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="standing-grants">Standing grants</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Approving the same
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">nomad job revert</code>
        for each of 20 allocations helps nobody. When you approve, choose a duration —
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">once</strong>
        for this run only, or a standing grant for
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">1 hour, 24 hours, 30 days, or 90 days</strong>
        — and matching future calls skip the queue:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Scoped tight.</strong>
            A grant is bound to one API key and action, and optionally one runner. When you
            approve, choose whether it covers only these exact arguments or any arguments.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Capped and revocable.</strong>
            A grant can carry a use limit, always expires, and is listed under
            <a
              href={~p"/app/approvals"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Approvals</a>
            where one click revokes it early. Every use is still a fully audited run.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="reason">The reason requirement</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Every dispatch — console or MCP — must include a <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">reason</code>.
        Approvers see it, and the run and audit event keep it. For an agent, the reason says
        why it ran <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">nomad job revert</code>;
        the action name only says what.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An MCP caller can also send
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">evidence</code>
        — prior findings or run IDs that informed the call — and
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">expected</code>
        — the outcome that confirms success. Together with the reason, they preserve what the
        agent saw, why it acted, and what it expected. Operator and runbook dispatches leave
        both empty.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
